    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's what I implemented for the `namesakeCount` rule.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe rule: on `POST /api/owners`, record how many existing owners already share the new owner's first + last name (case-insensitive), measured *before* this create, and return it on the owner.\n\n**Changes, each in the layer where it belongs:**\n\n1. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `CountNamesakes.java` (in the owner function package). A single-responsibility step that reads the built `Owner`, counts existing owners with a case-insensitive first+last name match via the existing `OwnerRepository.findByLastName` (filtered down to exact-name matches, mirroring how `EnsureUniqueHousehold` reuses that query), and stores the count on the entity.\n\n2. **Wiring** \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`: inserted `countNamesakes` between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance. It runs after `build` (so the `Owner` exists) and before `save` (so the count excludes the new owner \u2014 \"before this create\").\n\n3. **Persistence** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field + getter/setter and `@Column(name=\"namesake_count\")` to the `Owner` entity, and the `namesake_count` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql), since the value is read back later via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n4. **Contract** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` read-only integer property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`; MapStruct maps it automatically by name (getter \u2192 DTO field), so `RespondWith\u2026` and the GET responder need no changes.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreCountsNamesakes`) and the full suite are green.",
